The current position has three pieces for each player, with no immediate winning move available for \(\mathrm{x}\) (verified by testing all seven playable locations: none complete four consecutive \(\mathrm{x}\)). Likewise, \(\mathrm{o}\) has no immediate win and no move that creates a three-in-a-row possessing a playable fourth square; consequently there is no forced block.

The existing \(\mathrm{x}\) stones occupy \((5,3)\), \((3,3)\) and \((4,4)\). These stones already participate in two live four-in-a-row groups that can still be completed:

- vertical group on column 4, rows \(1\)–\(4\);
- several horizontal groups on row 3 (columns \(1\)–\(4\), \(2\)–\(5\) and \(3\)–\(6\)).

The unique square that simultaneously advances both families is \((3,4)\). Placing an \(\mathrm{x}\) there (column 4) produces two consecutive stones in each family:

- vertical pair \((3,4)\)-\((4,4)\), whose next square \((2,4)\) is immediately playable;
- horizontal pair \((3,3)\)-\((3,4)\), whose extensions lie on open files.

Because the two threats lie on different columns, a single reply by \(\mathrm{o}\) can neutralize at most one of them. If \(\mathrm{o}\) ignores the vertical, \(\mathrm{x}\) can play \((2,4)\) on the following turn and create a direct threat at \((1,4)\). If \(\mathrm{o}\) does occupy \((2,4)\), the horizontal pair on row 3 survives untouched and remains easy to extend later (especially rightward through column 5). In either branch \(\mathrm{x}\) obtains a lasting structural advantage.

All other columns are inferior:

- column 3 yields only a single (high) vertical pair that \(\mathrm{o}\) can cap at once;
- column 2 yields a bottom-row pair that \(\mathrm{o}\) can kill immediately by occupying column 1;
- column 5 opens the critical square \((3,5)\) of \(\mathrm{x}\)’s best diagonal, allowing \(\mathrm{o}\) to block that diagonal for free while giving \(\mathrm{x}\) only a dead horizontal pair;
- edge columns 0, 1 and 6 create no new connected pairs at all.

Thus column 4 is the unique move that maximises the number of live threats while preserving the initiative.

Final Answer: 4

